save(i,dict(title='Reachability in piecewise-affine integer dynamics',formal=r'Historical task, already classified in the source: given an explicitly represented piecewise-affine map \(f:\mathbb Z^n\to\mathbb Z^n\) and integer vectors \(x_0,y\), decide whether \(f^t(x_0)=y\) for some integer \(t\geq0\). There is no algorithm deciding all such inputs: the problem is recursively enumerable complete already in dimension two. In dimension one it is PSPACE-complete.',definitions=r'''The dimension \(n\geq1\) and a finite list of regions \(H_1,\ldots,H_p\) are given explicitly. The regions form a disjoint, exhaustive partition of \(\mathbb R^n\). Each region is a convex rational polyhedron specified by finitely many strict or non-strict rational linear inequalities. On integer points of region \(H_j\), the map is \(f(x)=A_jx+b_j\), where \(A_j\in\mathbb Z^{n\times n}\) and \(b_j\in\mathbb Z^n\). These integer coefficients guarantee an integer successor. There are no rounding operations or congruence tests.

All integer constants and rational numerators and denominators are written in binary. The input includes all regions, matrices, vectors and the starting and target vectors. Validity of the partition is a promise. The number of regions and coefficient magnitudes are not fixed constants.

Set \(f^0(x_0)=x_0\) and \(f^{t+1}(x_0)=f(f^t(x_0))\). Reachability includes time zero. The question concerns one given initial vector, unlike mortality, which requires every integer starting vector to reach zero.

“Recursively enumerable complete” means that yes-instances can be recognized by a procedure that halts when a target is found, but no total decision procedure exists; every recursively enumerable decision problem computably reduces to this one. PSPACE-completeness is for polynomial space in the total binary input length, with polynomial-time many-one reductions.

The source states the two-dimensional and one-dimensional classifications for target zero. They apply to any supplied integer target by translating coordinates: \(g(z)=f(z+y)-y\), with initial vector \(x_0-y\). Translation preserves the dimension, finite rational-polyhedral partition and integer affine coefficients, with polynomial encoding overhead. This is a direct consequence of the stated model, not a separate later theorem.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='The historical universal decidability question has a negative answer by the two-dimensional recursively enumerable completeness result. The one-dimensional PSPACE-complete restriction remains decidable. A solver for rational linear maps, a fixed bounded region count or another restricted update family does not decide the full piecewise-affine integer problem.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The imported sentence was a definition',text='Section 7 introduces orbit reachability immediately after stating its integer piecewise-affine complexity classification. The extraction mistook that introductory question for an unresolved problem. The actual open questions appear afterward and concern more restricted mortality tasks.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Dimension and update rules matter',text='The source reports PSPACE-completeness in one integer dimension and recursive-enumerability completeness in two. It separately recalls polynomial-time orbit reachability for a single rational linear transformation. These are different input families.',citation='primary')],why='A sharp boundary in verification of simple deterministic programs: one integer variable permits a decision procedure, while two already support undecidable reachability. The historical classification illustrates why integer state spaces, affine pieces and dimension must be stated explicitly.',progress=[dict(date='2013',text='The source states the complete one- and two-dimensional reachability classifications in its conclusion, before listing separate open questions.',citation='primary')],references=refs,status='resolved',status_note='Checked Definition 3 and the complete concluding passage. This was an erroneous open-question extraction, not a problem newly resolved after 2013. The source explicitly states the zero-target classification; coordinate translation supplies the given-target version. Later-source searches did not alter this historical disposition. No adjacent mortality question was substituted.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Replaced the extracted introductory sentence with its precise historical decision task and known status. Preserved the distinction between a given initial state and universal mortality.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A foundational decidability boundary for discrete dynamical systems and deterministic program reachability. Existing score retained as a historical assessment.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['Iterate a finite piecewise-affine integer map from a specified starting vector.','Ask whether one specified integer target is ever reached.','The source already states PSPACE-completeness in one dimension and undecidability in two.','Its extracted question introduced this known task rather than an open problem.','Separate questions about restricted universal mortality are not substituted here.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary'])),notes=['Recovered the integer coefficient and rational polyhedral partition model from Definition 3.','Verified that the original conclusion already classifies this given-start reachability task.','Recorded the elementary coordinate translation from arbitrary target to zero.','Marked the historical task resolved without replacing it with nearby open mortality questions.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
p=DIRECTORY/'queue.json';q=json.loads(p.read_text());_,c=card(i)
